<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Senegal River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> River flows from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> highlands to the Atlantic, forming much of the border between modern Senegal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>. Its valley was an important agricultural and trading zone at the southern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> profiles refer to it in describing the movements and territories of these states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/senegal-river.jpg" alt="Senegal River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WILLAV-FR · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WL-S%C3%A9n%C3%A9gal-Rosso-Travers%C3%A9e_du_fleuve_S%C3%A9n%C3%A9gal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Senegal River is 1086 kilometres long and drains a basin of 270,000 square kilometres. Its headwaters, the Bafing and the Semefé (Bakoye), both rise in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and meet at Bafoulabé in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>. From there the river flows west and then north through the Talari Gorges and over the Gouina Falls, past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayes">Kayes</a>, and receives the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faleme-river">Falémé River</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakel">Bakel</a>. It then crosses semi-arid land, forming the border between Senegal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, and takes in the Gorgol at Kaédi.</p><p>Below Kaédi it divides into two branches, the main river and the Doué, which rejoin a few kilometres downstream of Podor and enclose a long strip of land called the Île à Morfil. The river passes Richard Toll and Rosso and turns south around the island on which Saint-Louis stands, separated from the Atlantic by a thin strip of sand, the Langue de Barbarie.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> writers knew of the river: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> called it, or some other river, Bambotus, and Ptolemy called it Nias. Hanno the Carthaginian visited it around 450 BCE. From about 800 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trans-saharan-trade">Trans-Saharan trade</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a> restored contact, and the Arab geographers al-Masudi (957), al-Bakri (1068) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">al-Idrisi</a> (1154) gave some of the earliest descriptions. Some of them believed the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> formed a single river, the Western Nile, and later writers called it the Nile of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>. Because it reached into the gold-producing Ghana Empire and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>, traders called it the River of Gold, and European maps from the 14th century, among them the Hereford Mappa Mundi, drew it.</p><p>Europeans sought its mouth from 1291, when the Genoese Vivaldi brothers sailed down the coast and were not heard of again. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry the Navigator</a>, Gil Eanes passed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-bojador">Cape Bojador</a> in 1434. In 1445 Nuno Tristão reached the Langue de Barbarie, and Dinis Dias was the first known European since antiquity to reach the river's mouth. Between 1448 and 1455 the captain Lourenço Dias opened regular trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a> states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waalo">Waalo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor</a>, exchanging horses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> goods for gold and slaves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1972 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> founded the Organisation pour la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mise-en-valeur">mise en valeur</a> du fleuve Sénégal to manage the river basin, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> joined in 2005. Two large dams stand on the river: the Manantali Dam in Mali, and the Maka-Diama Dam on the Mauritania-Senegal border near the sea, which keeps salt water from moving upstream. The river's aquatic life is closely linked to that of the Gambia River basin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Senegal River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Within some seventy years they ruled a territory stretching roughly three thousand kilometres, from the <b>Senegal River</b> to the Ebro in northern Spain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>France had held Saint-Louis and Gorée on the Senegalese coast since the seventeenth century, and from the 1850s Governor Louis Faidherbe expanded the colony of Senegal up the <b>Senegal River</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Sundiata's death around 1255, his successors, among them Mansa Uli, expanded the empire towards the <b>Senegal River</b> and opened ties with the wider Islamic world; Uli made the pilgrimage to Mecca.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Serer people are held to have originated in the <b>Senegal River</b> valley, at the border of present-day Senegal and Mauritania, and to have moved south in the 11th and 12th centuries and again in the 15th and 16th.</blockquote><a href="/religion/serer-religion">Serer religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Majorcan sailor Jaume Ferrer set out from Majorca to find the &quot;River of Gold&quot;, the <b>Senegal River</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-10">10 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its territory lay around Aleg and along the right bank of the <b>Senegal River</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-brakna">Emirate of Brakna</a></li></ul></section>
